helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3154360				0	hy	aligning the elements in a matrix, for example \<emph\>matrix{alignr a#b+2##c+1/3#alignl d}\</emph\> and				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3155946				0	hy	beginning a new line, for example \<emph\>a+b-c newline alignr x/y\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3154621				0	hy	When using the align instructions, note that				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3147382				0	hy	they can only placed at the beginning of expressions and can only occur once. Therefore you can type \<emph\>a+b alignr c\</emph\>, but not \<emph\>a+alignr b\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3154004				0	hy	they affect each other, which means that typing \<emph\>{alignl{alignr a}}over{b+c}\</emph\> aligns \<emph\>a\</emph\> on the right.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	hd_id8036133				0	hy	To align using the "matrix" command				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_idN10F70				0	hy	Aligning to the left				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3149645				0	hy	If a line or an expression begins with text, it is aligned on the left by default. You can change this with any of the \<emph\>align\</emph\> commands. An example is \<emph\>stack{a+b-c*d#alignr "text"}\</emph\>, where "text" appears aligned to the right. Note that text must always be surrounded by quotation marks.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3149966				0	hy	The standard centralized formulas can be aligned to the left without using the \<emph\>Format - Alignment\</emph\> menu. To do this, place an empty character string, that is, the inverted commas which surround any text "", before the section of formula that you want to align. For example, typing \<emph\>"" a+b newline "" c+d\</emph\> results in both equations being left-aligned instead of centered.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3145654				0	hy	When typing information in the Commands window, note that some formats require spaces for the correct structure. This is especially true when entering values (for example, a lsup{3}) instead of placeholders.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3148708				0	hy	Click \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091100.xhp\" name=\"Brackets and Grouping\"\>Brackets and Grouping\</link\> for more information about formatting in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3155340				0	hy	Useful information about \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091200.xhp\" name=\"Indexes and Exponents\"\>Indexes and Exponents\</link\> and \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091400.xhp\" name=\"Scaling\"\>Scaling\</link\>, helps you organize your document in the best possible way.				20200411 15:09:53
