helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	tit				0	hy	About Layers				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149018				0	hy	\<bookmark_value\>layers; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149018				0	hy	\<variable id=\"layers\"\>\<link href=\"text/simpress/guide/layers.xhp\" name=\"About Layers\"\>About Layers\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3146313	3			0	hy	Layers are available in $[officename] Draw, not in $[officename] Impress. Layers allow you to assemble elements on a page that are related. Think of layers as individual workspaces that you can hide from view, hide from printing, or lock. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id7036957				0	hy	Layers do not determine the stacking order of objects on your page, except for the \<emph\>Controls\</emph\> layer which is always in front of other layers. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id1614734				0	hy	The stacking order of objects on your page is determined by the sequence in which you add the objects. You can rearrange the stacking order by \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify - Arrange\</item\>. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id398876				0	hy	The areas on a layer that do not contain objects are transparent.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3146962				0	hy	$[officename] Draw provides three default layers:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3153073				0	hy	Layout				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3149053				0	hy	Controls				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391				0	hy	Dimension Lines				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3156397				0	hy	You cannot delete or rename the default layers. You can add your own layers by \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Layer\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3150534				0	hy	The \<emph\>Layout\</emph\> layer is the default workspace. The \<emph\>Layout\</emph\> layer determines the location of title, text, and object placeholders on your page.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3150742				0	hy	The \<emph\>Controls\</emph\> layer can be used for buttons that have been assigned an action, but that should not be printed. Set the layer's properties to not printable. Objects on the \<emph\>Controls\</emph\> layer are always in front of objects on other layers.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3153085				0	hy	The \<emph\>Dimension Lines\</emph\> layer is where you draw, for example, the dimension lines. By switching the layer to show or hide, you can easily switch these lines on and off.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\layers.xhp	0	help	par_id3154507				0	hy	You can lock a layer to protect its contents, or hide a layer and its contents from view or from printing. When you add a new layer to a page, the layer is added to all of the pages in your document. However, when you add an object to a layer, it is only added to the current page. If you want the object to appear on all of the pages, add the object to the master page (\<item type=\"menuitem\"\>View - Master\</item\>).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	tit				0	hy	Importing HTML Pages Into Presentations 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146121				0	hy	\<bookmark_value\>importing; presentations with HTML\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>presentations; importing HTML\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>HTML; importing into presentations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>text documents;inserting in slides\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting; text in presentations\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146121				0	hy	\<variable id=\"html_import\"\>\<link href=\"text/simpress/guide/html_import.xhp\" name=\"Importing HTML Pages Into Presentations \"\>Importing HTML Pages Into Presentations \</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	par_id3150750				0	hy	You can import any text file, including text in HTML documents, into a slide.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155443				0	hy	To insert text from a file into a slide:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	par_id3146313				0	hy	In the slide where you want to insert the text, choose \<emph\>Insert - File\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	par_id3150207				0	hy	Select "Text" or "HTML Document" as the \<emph\>File type\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	par_id3148610				0	hy	Locate the file containing the text that you want to add, and then click \<emph\>Insert\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	par_id3153915				0	hy	If the text file contains more text than can be inserted into a single slide, you can divide the text over several slides.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	par_id3149126				0	hy	Double-click in the inserted text to enter edit mode.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	par_id3143228				0	hy	Select all of the text that lies below the visible slide area and press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+X.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	par_id3153811				0	hy	Choose \<emph\>Insert – Slide\</emph\>, and then press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+V.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_import.xhp	0	help	par_id3147297				0	hy	Repeat steps 1 to 3 until all of the text is on slides.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_export.xhp	0	help	tit				0	hy	Saving a Presentation in HTML Format				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_export.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155067				0	hy	\<bookmark_value\>exporting;presentations to HTML\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>saving;as HTML\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>presentations; exporting to HTML\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>HTML; exporting from presentations\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_export.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155067				0	hy	\<variable id=\"html_export\"\>\<link href=\"text/simpress/guide/html_export.xhp\" name=\"Saving a Presentation in HTML Format\"\>Saving a Presentation in HTML Format\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_export.xhp	0	help	par_id3153246				0	hy	Open the presentation that you want to save in HTML format.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_export.xhp	0	help	par_id3149502				0	hy	Choose \<emph\>File - Export\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_export.xhp	0	help	par_id3148842				0	hy	Set the \<emph\>File type\</emph\> to \<emph\>HTML Document ($[officename] Impress) (.html;.htm)\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\html_export.xhp	0	help	par_id3143228				0	hy	Enter a \<emph\>File name\</emph\>, and then click \<emph\>Export\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
